{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.BerezinianSelfAdjointCrossTerm","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.BerezinianSelfAdjointCrossTerm","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.ZBarParity"],"declarations":2,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.BerezinianSelfAdjointCrossTerm.barOdd_crossTerm_forbids_selfadjoint","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.BerezinianSelfAdjointCrossTerm.barOdd_crossTerm_forbids_selfadjoint","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BerezinianSelfAdjointCrossTerm.lean#L59-L74","doc":"**A bar-ODD cross-term forbids graded self-adjointness (modulo 2-torsion).**\nContrapositive of `selfadjoint_crossTerm_bar_even`: if the cross-term\n`t = B·Dinv·C` is bar-ODD (`σ t = −t`, the parity `c₃` must have) with `Dinv`\nbar-even, then the self-adjointness relation `C = −σ B` forces `2·t = 0`. So over a\n2-torsion-free ring a **genuinely** bar-odd cross-term cannot arise from\nbar-conjugate (self-adjoint) off-blocks — the off-diagonal blocks must be\nindependent. This is the constraint the odd-face `g·γ` model implicitly requires. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.BerezinianSelfAdjointCrossTerm.selfadjoint_crossTerm_bar_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.BerezinianSelfAdjointCrossTerm.selfadjoint_crossTerm_bar_even","kind":"theorem","line":45,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BerezinianSelfAdjointCrossTerm.lean#L45-L57","doc":"**The graded-self-adjoint Berezinian cross-term is bar-EVEN.** If the odd\noff-diagonal blocks are bar-conjugate (`C = −σ B`, the graded-self-adjointness\ncondition) and the even inverse-block `Dinv` is bar-even (`σ Dinv = Dinv`), then the\nscalar cross-term `t = B·Dinv·C = −B·Dinv·σB` satisfies `σ t = t` — it is bar-EVEN\n(a `|B|²/D` norm-squared object), for **any** ring involution `σ` (`σ∘σ = id`). This\nis the opposite of the `OddDecayWeightParity` assumption. ","signatureChunk":0}]}